actors: Agent runner | Lab IdP | This app | OPA

step: The agent asks for a token with the scopes it needs
msg: Agent runner -> Lab IdP: POST /token, scopes
text: Same client credentials as lab 07. The agent asks for `stats:read` and
  `reports:write`, and the IdP only grants what it was registered for.

step: The IdP writes the allowed actions into the token
note: Lab IdP: adds capabilities
msg: Lab IdP -> Agent runner: token + actions
text: Each capability names one tool and any limits on it, and is only included
  when its scope was granted. Ask for fewer scopes and you carry fewer
  capabilities.

step: The runner sends a tool call to one door
msg: Agent runner -> This app: POST /agent/tools/write_report
text: Lab 07 had a route per tool. Now every tool goes through the gateway,
  with the arguments in the body. That gives the policy one place to look.

step: The app asks the IdP what the token says
msg: This app -> Lab IdP: POST /introspect
text: The app trusts nothing in the request except the token, and only through
  the IdP's answer. The capabilities come back from here, not from the caller.

step: The app builds a question for OPA
msg: Lab IdP -> This app: active + grants
note: This app: builds input
text: The input holds who is asking, what the token allows and which tool is
  being called with which arguments. Only the tool and its arguments come
  from the request.

step: OPA evaluates the Rego policy
msg: This app -> OPA: POST /decision
note: OPA: runs the rules
text: Every rule that objects adds a named entry to `deny`. One entry is
  enough to block the call, whatever else would have allowed it.

step: OPA answers with the policies that decided
msg: OPA -> This app: allow/deny + names
text: The answer lists the policy names, such as `report-too-long`, not just
  yes or no. If the app can't read a clear yes, it treats the answer as no.

step: The app records the decision and then acts on it
note: This app: audit with policy name
msg: This app -> Agent runner: 200, or 403 + policy
text: The audit row carries the denying policy, so "why was it refused" has a
  one-line answer. Only on an allow does the tool actually run.
